'<!-- Side-by-side comparison of the same function expanded by classical Fourier-coefficient integration and by the discrete FFT.
'Highlights where the two reconstructions agree and how the sample count $n$ controls FFT-based aliasing. -->
'<h1>Fourier series expansion (classic)</h1>
#rad
#UI 'Number of iterations -'n = 20
#UI 'Interval length -'l = 10
#UI 'Number of samples -'n_s = 32
#pause
'Function -'f(x) = (e^x - x^2 - 2*x^4)/l
'Coefficients -'a_0 = 2/l*$Integral{f(x) @ x = 0 : l}
a(k) = 2/l*$Integral{f(x)*cos(2*π*k/l*x) @ x = 0 : l}', ' _
b(k) = 2/l*$Integral{f(x)*sin(2*π*k/l*x) @ x = 0 : l}
'Expansion to series
f_s(x) = a_0/2 + $Sum{a(k)*cos(2*π*k/l*x) + b(k)*sin(2*π*k/l*x) @ k = 1 : n}
'Plot the function and Fourier approximation
$Plot{f(x) & f_s(x) @ x = 0 : l}
'<h1>Fourier series expansion (FFT)</h1>
'<hr/>
'Sampling step -'dx = l/n_s
'Sample points -'x = range(0; n_s; 1)*dx
'Apply FFT -'y = fft(hp([f(x)|0]))
'Fourier coefficients from FFT:
a_0 = y.(1; 1)/n_s'- first coefficient
a(k) = y.(1; k + 1)/n_s'- cosine coefficients, ' _
b(k) = -(y.(2; k + 1)/n_s)'-sine coefficients
x_t(x) = l - x/2'- abscissa scalling
'Function reconstruction
f_fft(x) = a_0/2 + $Sum{a(k)*cos(2*π*k/l*x_t(x)) + b(k)*sin(2*π*k/l*x_t(x)) @ k = 1 : n_s}
'Plot the function and its FFT-based Fourier approximation
$Plot{f(x) & f_fft(x) @ x = 0 : l}
